SHOPGRAPH — PURCHASE HISTORY ANALYTICS DASHBOARD

GOAL

Add a simple, reliable analytics capability to ShopGraph that reads:

    data/database/shopgraph_purchase_history.xlsx

and creates or completely rebuilds a visible Excel worksheet named:

    Analytics

containing seven useful spending charts based on the first worksheet:

    Purchase History

This is a household / shopping expense dashboard.

Do NOT build complicated financial models.
Do NOT add forecasting.
Do NOT use macros.
Do NOT use Excel PivotTables or PivotCharts.
Do NOT require pandas.

Prefer straightforward Python + openpyxl calculations and native Excel charts.

The goal is:

    useful
    understandable
    maintainable
    deterministic
    reliable


============================================================
CURRENT PURCHASE HISTORY STRUCTURE
============================================================

The source worksheet is:

    Purchase History

The current ShopGraph schema is:

    A   Total
    B   <blank spacer>
    C   Store
    D   Six-Digit SKU
    E   Product
    F   Tax Code
    G   Store Number
    H   Common Name
    I   Category
    J   Date 1
    K   Price 1
    L   Date 2
    M   Price 2
    N   Date 3
    O   Price 3
    ...

Date / Price pairs continue dynamically.

Do NOT assume that there is only one Date/Price pair.

The analytics code must dynamically discover all headers matching:

    Date N
    Price N

and pair:

    Date 1 -> Price 1
    Date 2 -> Price 2
    Date 3 -> Price 3
    etc.


============================================================
CRITICAL DATA RULE
============================================================

For analytics calculations, DO NOT depend on the value in the Excel:

    Total

column.

The workbook Total field may contain an Excel formula.

openpyxl does not calculate Excel formulas itself, so cached values may be
missing or stale.

Instead, construct analytics directly from the individual:

    Date N + Price N

purchase observations.

Every valid Date N / Price N pair represents one purchase observation.


============================================================
NORMALIZED ANALYTICS RECORD
============================================================

Internally flatten Purchase History into records conceptually equivalent to:

{
    "date": datetime,
    "price": float,
    "store": "Aldi",
    "store_number": "160",
    "product": "Zucchini",
    "common_name": "Zucchini",
    "category": "Fresh Vegetables",
    "six_digit_sku": "356691"
}

For example, if one Purchase History row contains:

    Store = Aldi
    Product = Zucchini
    Category = Fresh Vegetables
    Date 1 = 08/23/2026
    Price 1 = 2.52
    Date 2 = 09/05/2026
    Price 2 = 2.69

it creates TWO analytics observations.

This flattened representation should be the source for all charts.


============================================================
VALID PURCHASE OBSERVATION
============================================================

Only include a Date N / Price N pair when:

1. Date is a valid Excel date, datetime, or recognizable date string.
2. Price is a valid non-negative numeric value.
3. Both Date N and its corresponding Price N exist.

Skip malformed/incomplete pairs safely.

Do not crash because one cell contains:

    NA
    blank
    malformed text
    formula
    None

Print a short warning count if observations were skipped.


============================================================
NA HANDLING
============================================================

Treat the following as unavailable:

    None
    ""
    "NA"

For grouping fields:

If Store is unavailable:

    Store = "Unknown"

If Category is unavailable:

    Category = "Uncategorized"

If Common Name is unavailable:

    first fall back to Product.

If Product is also unavailable:

    Product = "Unknown Product"

Do not discard otherwise-valid spending observations merely because Category,
Common Name, SKU, or Store Number is unavailable.


============================================================
OUTPUT WORKSHEET
============================================================

Create a visible worksheet named:

    Analytics

If Analytics already exists:

    DELETE it and regenerate it completely.

This ensures the dashboard never becomes stale or accumulates duplicate
charts.

Place Analytics after Purchase History if practical.

Do NOT modify purchase-history data while generating analytics.


============================================================
HELPER DATA
============================================================

Charts will require aggregate tables.

Keep the visible Analytics worksheet clean.

Preferred approach:

Create a second worksheet named:

    _AnalyticsData

containing calculated helper tables.

Set:

    _AnalyticsData.sheet_state = "hidden"

If _AnalyticsData already exists:

    delete and regenerate it.

All chart source ranges should reference _AnalyticsData.

Do NOT expose large calculation tables on the visible dashboard.


============================================================
DASHBOARD HEADER
============================================================

At the top of Analytics create:

    ShopGraph Spending Analytics

Below it show a small summary area containing:

    Total Spending
    Purchase Observations
    Date Range
    Number of Stores
    Number of Categories

Definitions:

Total Spending:
    sum of every valid Price N observation

Purchase Observations:
    number of valid Date/Price pairs

Date Range:
    earliest date through latest date

Number of Stores:
    unique known stores

Number of Categories:
    unique known categories excluding Uncategorized if possible

Format Total Spending as currency.

Example:

    ShopGraph Spending Analytics

    Total Spending:          $1,428.37
    Purchase Observations:   214
    Date Range:              08/23/2026 - 11/30/2026
    Stores:                  4
    Categories:              19


============================================================
THE SEVEN CHARTS
============================================================


------------------------------------------------------------
CHART 1 — MONTHLY SPENDING TREND
------------------------------------------------------------

Title:

    Monthly Spending

Chart type:

    LineChart

Purpose:

Show whether overall spending is increasing or decreasing over time.

Group all purchase observations by calendar month.

Use:

    YYYY-MM

or an actual first-of-month Excel date displayed as:

    mmm yyyy

Y-axis:

    Spending ($)

X-axis:

    Month

Sort chronologically.

Do not fill missing months incorrectly.

Prefer including zero-spend months between the earliest and latest observed
months so the time series does not visually skip time.

Example helper table:

    Month          Spending
    Aug 2026       482.31
    Sep 2026       394.20
    Oct 2026       551.72


------------------------------------------------------------
CHART 2 — SPENDING BY CATEGORY
------------------------------------------------------------

Title:

    Spending by Category

Chart type:

    Horizontal BarChart

Purpose:

Show which types of goods consume the most money.

Aggregate:

    SUM(price)
    GROUP BY Category

Sort from highest spend to lowest spend.

Include all meaningful categories.

If there are more than approximately 15 categories:

    show Top 14
    combine remaining categories into "Other"

Y-axis:

    Category

X-axis:

    Spending ($)

A bar chart is preferred over a pie chart because many categories are easier
to compare as bars.


------------------------------------------------------------
CHART 3 — SPENDING BY STORE
------------------------------------------------------------

Title:

    Spending by Store

Chart type:

    Horizontal BarChart

Purpose:

Show which retailers receive the most spending.

Aggregate:

    SUM(price)
    GROUP BY Store

Sort highest to lowest.

Examples:

    Aldi
    Publix
    Trader Joe's
    Other

Do not split one store by Store Number for this chart.

Store Number is intentionally ignored here.

X-axis:

    Spending ($)


------------------------------------------------------------
CHART 4 — CATEGORY SHARE OF SPENDING
------------------------------------------------------------

Title:

    Spending Share by Category

Chart type:

    DoughnutChart

Purpose:

Provide a quick visual sense of the household spending mix.

Because pie/doughnut charts become unreadable with too many slices:

Use:

    Top 7 categories by spending

and combine every remaining category into:

    Other

Show percentage data labels if openpyxl supports them reliably.

Do not create dozens of slices.

Do not use 3-D pie charts.

This chart intentionally complements Chart 2:

Chart 2 provides precise category comparison.
Chart 4 provides quick proportional understanding.


------------------------------------------------------------
CHART 5 — TOP 10 PRODUCTS BY SPENDING
------------------------------------------------------------

Title:

    Top 10 Products by Spending

Chart type:

    Horizontal BarChart

Purpose:

Identify the individual products responsible for the most accumulated
spending.

Product identity priority:

    Common Name
        ↓
    Product
        ↓
    "Unknown Product"

Aggregate all historical purchase observations for the same selected identity.

Example:

    Zucchini
    Ground Turkey
    Gluten-Free Bread
    Yogurt
    Soymilk

Sort highest to lowest.

Display only the Top 10.

X-axis:

    Spending ($)

IMPORTANT:

Do not simply use the current product price.

Sum ALL historical Date N / Price N observations belonging to the product.


------------------------------------------------------------
CHART 6 — MONTHLY SPENDING BY STORE
------------------------------------------------------------

Title:

    Monthly Spending by Store

Chart type:

    Stacked ColumnChart

Purpose:

Show both:

1. total spending over time
2. which stores contributed to that spending

Rows:

    Month

Columns:

    Store

Values:

    SUM(price)

Example helper table:

    Month       Aldi    Publix    Trader Joe's    Other
    Aug 2026    210      170          82             20
    Sep 2026    165      105          94             30

Sort months chronologically.

If there are more than 6 stores:

    retain the Top 5 stores by lifetime spending
    combine the rest into "Other"

This prevents the chart from becoming visually noisy.


------------------------------------------------------------
CHART 7 — PURCHASE FREQUENCY BY CATEGORY
------------------------------------------------------------

Title:

    Purchase Frequency by Category

Chart type:

    Horizontal BarChart

Purpose:

Distinguish categories purchased frequently from categories that merely cost
a lot.

Aggregate:

    COUNT(valid Date N / Price N observations)
    GROUP BY Category

This is NOT spending.

It represents number of purchase observations.

Example:

    Fresh Vegetables    48
    Fresh Fruit         31
    Yogurt              14
    Bread                9

Sort highest to lowest.

If there are more than 15 categories:

    show Top 14
    combine the remainder into "Other"

X-axis:

    Purchases

This chart is valuable because:

    high spending + low frequency

means something different from:

    moderate spending + very high frequency


============================================================
WHY THESE SEVEN
============================================================

The dashboard deliberately answers seven simple questions:

1. How much am I spending over time?
       Monthly Spending

2. What kinds of things consume the most money?
       Spending by Category

3. Where am I spending the money?
       Spending by Store

4. What proportion of spending goes to each major category?
       Spending Share by Category

5. Which individual products are my biggest spending drivers?
       Top 10 Products by Spending

6. How does my store mix change over time?
       Monthly Spending by Store

7. What categories do I buy most often?
       Purchase Frequency by Category

Do NOT add more graphs merely because data exists.

Seven useful charts are preferable to a complicated dashboard.


