LAYOUT
============================================================

Create a clean dashboard layout.

Suggested placement:

    A1:N5
        Dashboard title + summary statistics

    A7:G22
        Chart 1 — Monthly Spending

    H7:N22
        Chart 4 — Category Share

    A24:G40
        Chart 2 — Spending by Category

    H24:N40
        Chart 3 — Spending by Store

    A42:G58
        Chart 5 — Top 10 Products

    H42:N58
        Chart 7 — Purchase Frequency

    A60:N80
        Chart 6 — Monthly Spending by Store

The exact dimensions may be adjusted if necessary for readability.

Avoid charts overlapping each other.

Set sensible widths/heights.


============================================================
FORMATTING
============================================================

Keep formatting professional but simple.

Use:

    bold dashboard title
    bold summary labels
    currency number formats
    readable chart titles
    axis titles where useful
    legends only when useful

Do not rely on custom external fonts.

Do not make the dashboard overly decorative.

Avoid 3-D charts.

Avoid excessive colors or manual per-point coloring.

Use standard Excel/openpyxl chart styling.


============================================================
DYNAMIC BEHAVIOR
============================================================

The analytics generator must work when:

    new products are added
    new stores are added
    new categories are added
    Date 2 / Price 2 appears
    Date 3 / Price 3 appears
    Date 20 / Price 20 eventually appears

Never hard-code:

    J/K only

or a maximum Date/Price pair count.

Discover the history columns from row 1 every time the script runs.


============================================================
DATE HANDLING
============================================================

Dates may exist as:

    Python datetime
    Python date
    Excel date/datetime
    MM/DD/YYYY strings

Normalize them internally.

Do not group alphabetically by month name.

Always group chronologically by:

    year + month

This is important when history spans multiple years.


============================================================
PRICE HANDLING
============================================================

Prices may be:

    int
    float
    numeric string

Convert safely to float.

Reject:

    NA
    blank
    negative values
    malformed values

Do not count an invalid price as $0 unless it truly represents a numeric zero.


============================================================
RELIABILITY RULES
============================================================

1. Never modify Purchase History cells.
2. Never modify Imported Receipts.
3. Analytics must be reproducible entirely from Purchase History.
4. Regenerating Analytics should give the same result for the same workbook.
5. Do not depend on cached values from Total formulas.
6. Derive spending directly from Date N / Price N pairs.
7. Handle NA values safely.
8. Handle missing categories/stores safely.
9. Do not crash on a malformed individual history cell.
10. Preserve the workbook's existing worksheets and data.
11. Save atomically if consistent with the existing ShopGraph workbook code.
12. Do not corrupt the workbook if chart generation fails.


============================================================
EMPTY / SMALL DATASETS
============================================================

The feature must work even when there are very few purchase observations.

If there is no valid purchase history:

Create Analytics with:

    ShopGraph Spending Analytics

    No purchase history is available yet.

Do not create broken charts.

If a particular chart has insufficient data:

write a short note in its intended area, for example:

    Not enough data for Monthly Spending by Store.

Do not create an invalid chart.


============================================================
IMPLEMENTATION LOCATION
============================================================

Keep analytics separate from receipt-import/database-writing logic.

Suggested file:

    utils/DataBaseBuilder/excel/purchase_analytics.py

Suggested public function:

    generate_purchase_analytics() -> Path

or:

    generate_purchase_analytics(
        workbook_path: Path = WORKBOOK_PATH,
    ) -> Path

Use:

    openpyxl

because the existing Purchase History implementation already uses openpyxl.

Do not add pandas unless there is a compelling technical reason.

There should not be one for these aggregations.


============================================================
MENU INTEGRATION
============================================================

Add a second item to:

    === ShopGraph Data Base Builder ===

Current:

    1. Add Receipt to Purchase History
    0. Return to Main

Change to:

    1. Add Receipt to Purchase History
    2. Generate / Refresh Purchase Analytics
    0. Return to Main

Selecting option 2 should:

1. Open shopgraph_purchase_history.xlsx
2. Read Purchase History
3. Delete/rebuild Analytics
4. Delete/rebuild _AnalyticsData
5. Save the workbook
6. Print something similar to:

    [OK] Purchase Analytics refreshed.

    Workbook:
    <path>

    Charts created: 7

Do NOT automatically generate analytics every time a receipt is imported.

Keep it an explicit menu operation for now.

This reduces complexity and makes debugging easier.

A future change can automatically refresh Analytics after every receipt if
desired.


============================================================
OPTIONAL NICE TOUCH
============================================================

Freeze panes on Analytics if useful.

Hide gridlines on the Analytics sheet:

    sheet.sheet_view.showGridLines = False

Hide _AnalyticsData:

    data_sheet.sheet_state = "hidden"

Do not protect the sheets with passwords.


============================================================
TESTING
============================================================

Test at minimum:

1. Workbook with one Date 1 / Price 1 pair.
2. Workbook with multiple Date N / Price N pairs.
3. Multiple stores.
4. Multiple categories.
5. NA Category.
6. NA Store.
7. NA Common Name.
8. Common Name falling back to Product.
9. Multiple years.
10. Malformed date.
11. Malformed price.
12. Existing Analytics sheet.
13. Existing _AnalyticsData sheet.
14. Empty Purchase History.
15. More than 7 categories.
16. More than 6 stores.
17. More than 10 products.

Verify mathematically that:

    dashboard total spending
        =
    sum of every valid Price N cell paired with a valid Date N

Verify that monthly totals sum back to the same overall total.

Verify that category totals sum back to the same overall total.

Verify that store totals sum back to the same overall total.


